-- T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT, THE TYPE ARM (#1455 round-4 adversarial review, F1).
--
-- `Wrap` has TWO named-field constructors and they declare `alpha` at the SAME
-- offset (0 in both) but at DIFFERENT TYPES.  The offset test that shipped in round
-- 4 is structurally unable to see this shape, and round 4's comment claimed the
-- offset test was "the EXACT characterization of the newly-reachable damage, not a
-- proxy for it".  It was not.  This is the counterexample, and it is the exact
-- loud → silent severity increase the guard exists to prevent:
--
--     merge base `e6392c19`  `check` exit 1 — `Type mismatch: Wrap vs Aaa` x2
--     round 4 (`fd2c5ecb`)   `check` exit 0 with ZERO diagnostics, `build` exit 0,
--                            and the BUILT BINARY printed `c=7 d=1 a=8` — `Dfg`'s
--                            `True` read as an `Int`, at exit 0.  `medaka run`
--                            raised `E-PANIC intToString: not an Int`, i.e. it was
--                            RIGHT and therefore blind: the #1216 signature.
--
-- With the rival field spelled `String` instead of `Bool` the same program printed a
-- HEAP ADDRESS as an `Int` — three runs, three different addresses — which is why
-- the pinned spelling is `Bool`: it is deterministic.
--
-- ⚠️ THE THIRD DECLARATION IS NOT DECORATION — IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS NEWLY
-- REACHABLE.  `Aaa` shares both field names with `Wrap`'s constructors and has
-- nothing else to do with them.  Without it the merge base accepts the program too
-- (and miscompiles it identically), so the shape would be pre-existing rather than
-- something #1455 must not ship: it is the collider that puts `alpha` under two
-- registry owners, which is what #1455's Q-IDENT selection made resolvable instead
-- of a spurious `Type mismatch`.  Delete `Aaa` and this cell stops testing #1455.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE TYPE ARM IS NOT COVERED BY THE UNDECIDABILITY ARGUMENT that keeps
-- `field_variant_offset_agree_control.mdk` legal.  That argument is about WHICH
-- CONSTRUCTOR REACHES the access, which is runtime dataflow.  "The two constructors
-- declare this field at different types" is a DECLARATION-level fact, and it is
-- stronger than a layout disagreement: the selection is choosing the expression's
-- own STATIC TYPE arbitrarily, so the program has no meaning for any back end to
-- get right, whichever constructor turns up.
--
-- ⚠️ THE SECOND HALF, `Mixed`, IS THE PARAMETRIC SPELLING OF THE SAME DEFECT, AND IT
-- IS HERE BECAUSE IT IS THE ONE A NAIVE TYPE TEST MISSES.  The RAW declared types are
-- a type PARAMETER against `Int`, which any variable-tolerant comparison declines on
-- — so a first cut of this rule accepted it.  MEASURED on this branch at that point,
-- on the same three-module shape as `Wrap`: `check` exit 0, `build` exit 0, and the
-- built binary SEGFAULTED at exit 139 reading the `Int` as a `String`, where the merge
-- base rejected it at exit 1.  A second loud → silent, one axis over from `Wrap`'s.
-- The shipped rule instantiates each constructor's declared type at the RECEIVER's
-- type arguments first (`monoAtParams`), so `Mixed String` is a conflict here while
-- the SAME declarations at `Mixed Int` are not — which is what
-- `field_variant_param_type_control.mdk` pins.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ASSERTION IS THE ERROR THAT DOES APPEAR, NOT THE ABSENCE OF ONE — the same
-- trick the sibling controls use.  Here the golden IS the conflict message, so no
-- unrelated ill-typing is needed; what has to be checked by eye is that each message
-- names the two TYPES (not two positions, which agree in both halves) and that the
-- `help` offers the type remedy rather than the re-ordering one.
data Wrap =
  | Cfg { alpha : Int, beta : Int }
  | Dfg { alpha : Bool, beta : Int }

data Aaa = { alpha : Int, beta : Int }

data Mixed m =
  | Mx { mf : m, mg : Int }
  | My { mf : Int, mg : Int }

data Amix = { mf : Int, mg : Int }

getAlpha : Wrap -> Int
getAlpha w = w.alpha

getMf : Mixed String -> String
getMf m = m.mf
